From da6f66b50aa0796a33fc0cb549b7d3e62e3a685a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HiGarfield <HiGarfield@126.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:20:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/21] fix(robustness): create missing lock and pid
 directories

p910nd could not start on any image whose lock directory does not exist.
get_lock() opens LOCKFILE directly, so on a host without /var/lock/subsys
-- the normal state on Debian/Ubuntu and on most read-only or diskless
hosts -- open() failed with ENOENT, get_lock() returned failure and the
daemon exited immediately.  This is Debian bug #634225 and it has never
been merged here.  The standalone daemon had the identical failure for
/var/run/PIDFILE.

Add ensure_parent_dir(), which creates every directory component before
the final one, and call it for the lock file and the pid file.

The documented behaviour is unchanged: the lock name, its mode, the
flock() semantics and every existing path stay exactly as they were, and
a directory that already exists is left untouched.  The only difference
is that a missing directory no longer aborts startup.  The paths come
from compile-time macros rather than from the command line, so no
untrusted input reaches mkdir(), and failures are still reported by the
following open()/fopen(), which prints the real reason.

Regression test: tests/functional.py t_lock_dir_created, which runs a
daemon whose compiled-in LOCKFILE_DIR is absent and asserts it starts;
it failed before this change and passes after it.

Fixes BUG-001 (Debian #634225).
