From a94b8cbfef0f1800c3a519d67e9ec553e2812460 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HiGarfield <HiGarfield@126.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:29:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 03/21] fix(lock): retry the blocking lock wait when
 interrupted by a signal

get_lock() used F_SETLKW and treated any fcntl() failure as fatal.  That
wait returns -1/EINTR when a signal is delivered while it blocks, so a
single signal landing at the wrong moment made the daemon abandon a lock
it could have taken on the next attempt.  Under (x)inetd several
instances routinely wait for their turn on this very call, so the
consequence was either a daemon exiting for no reason or one proceeding
without the mutual exclusion the lock exists to provide.

Loop the wait: only an error other than EINTR fails.

This is a guard rather than a bug reachable today -- nothing in the
current build installs a handler that interrupts the wait (SIGPIPE is
ignored) -- but it becomes live the moment any signal handler is added
and on C libraries whose signal() does not set SA_RESTART.

tests/test_lock_eintr_retry.c reproduces it deterministically: a child
holds the lock, the parent waits with a non-restarting SIGALRM armed, and
get_lock() must retry instead of failing.  Verified by mutation:
reverting the retry to 'if (1)' makes the new assert fail, restoring it
makes the test pass.

tests/run.sh: compile unit tests with -DLOCKFILE_DIR so lock-touching
tests write to the scratch directory rather than the host's /var/lock.

Fixes BUG-002.
