tests/test_onejob_lock_exit.c forced get_lock() to fail by pointing
LOCKFILE_DIR at a directory that did not exist.  BUG-001 made that
directory get created, so the lock now succeeded: one_job() no longer
took its failure branch and instead blocked forever retrying
open_printer() for /dev/tty, and the test timed out (exit 124).

Induce the failure in a way directory creation cannot repair instead: put
an ordinary file in the middle of the path, so open() of the lock file
fails with ENOTDIR and get_lock() returns 0 exactly as intended.

The assertion is unchanged and still has teeth: reverting one_job()'s
exit(1) back to a bare return makes this test fail, so it continues to
cover the silent-return bug it was written for.
