From: HiGarfield <HiGarfield@126.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 02:02:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 08/21] fix(pidfile): refuse symlinked pid files and remove the
 file when stopping

Two long-standing weaknesses in the standalone pid file:

1. It was written with fopen(name, "w"), which follows symbolic links.  The
   daemon normally runs as root, so a symlink planted by an unprivileged user
   would have made startup truncate whatever that link pointed at.  open()
   with O_NOFOLLOW now refuses instead.  O_EXCL is deliberately absent: a pid
   file left by a crash must be overwritten, or every later start would fail.
   The pid is formatted as long because pid_t need not be int.

2. It was never removed, so /var/run/p9100d.pid kept naming a PID that no
   longer exists -- and which a later, unrelated process may recycle.  Any
   script acting on it would then signal the wrong process.  SIGTERM/SIGINT
   are now handled (flag only; cleanup runs in the loop where it is safe),
   installed without SA_RESTART so they interrupt a blocking accept(), and
   the pid file is unlinked on the way out; atexit() covers the other
   orderly exits.

PIDFILE is now overridable at compile time like LOCKFILE and PRINTERFILE,
so distributions can relocate it and the path is testable without root.

Tests: pidfile_removed_on_termination (file appears while running, gone
after SIGTERM) and pidfile_symlink_refused (symlinked pid file is not
followed -- its target keeps its contents -- and the daemon does not come
up).  Mutation-checked: dropping O_NOFOLLOW makes the second test report
the target clobbered.

tests/functional.py also pins the daemon's stdin to /dev/null: the runner
here hands the process a socket as descriptor 0, which made is_standalone()
succeed and silently send the daemon down the inetd path, so no pid file
was ever written.  Teardown now also reaps the daemonized pid -- the daemon
calls setsid(), so it escapes the process-group kill and was previously
left holding the port for the next case.

Resolves U4.
