From d7192b5a84f2e66a268f2f244d36f1e8725f3ba3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HiGarfield <HiGarfield@126.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 02:04:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 09/21] feat(security): optional privilege dropping with -u/-g

The daemon traditionally runs as root for its whole lifetime even though
the only things that genuinely need privilege are the lock file, the pid
file and binding the listening socket.  Add -u <user> and -g <group>,
each optional and defaulting to no change, so an operator whose printer
node is accessible to an unprivileged account can stop running the whole
thing as root.

Names and numeric ids are both accepted.  The change is applied after the
privileged setup: standalone drops once the lock file, pid file and
listening socket exist; under (x)inetd it drops right after the lock.
Supplementary groups are only initialised when privilege is still held, so
a daemon that is already unprivileged may simply ask for the identity it
has.  An unknown user or group is a hard error rather than something to
ignore -- silently continuing as root would be the worst outcome.

The documented trade-off: the printer device is re-opened for every job,
so after dropping privileges the target account must be able to open it.
That is recorded in the man page rather than papered over.

Tests: dropping to the caller's own identity still serves a byte-exact
job (and the log proves the switch happened), and bogus -u/-g are
refused.  Mutation-checked: making drop_privileges() return early makes
the first test fail.

Resolves U2.
