From 1097fd4e5278551d8f232d64f3bc8760da1d7ea9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HiGarfield <HiGarfield@126.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 02:09:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/21] fix(robustness): open the printer before accepting a
 connection

server() used to accept() first and only then retry open_printer() every
10 seconds.  While the device was missing, that connection was accepted
but could not be served -- and since this daemon handles one job at a
time, no other client could take its place either.  One client, or one
mistyped -f argument, was enough to make the daemon unreachable until the
printer reappeared.

Open the printer first and accept only once it is there.  Retrying
forever -- what hotplug and network printers need -- is unchanged, and
connect() still succeeds immediately because the kernel completes the
handshake into the listen backlog; the job is simply served when the
device appears.  The device descriptor is released on every path that
does not lead to a job, including a rejected libwrap client, so nothing
leaks while waiting.

Trade-off, deliberate: the device is now opened slightly earlier, so a
printer that disappears while a job is still queued fails that job; the
next iteration reopens it.  That is the price of not letting one client
hold the daemon.

Test: missing_printer_not_accepted_first asserts the daemon listens with
the device absent, logs no accepted connection, and then serves the
queued job intact once the device is created.  Mutation-checked:
accepting regardless of the device makes it fail.

