From 55560c5282e2b7011ba112c4d8e72e829f193a70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HiGarfield <HiGarfield@126.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 02:11:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 11/21] feat(security): optional build-time allowlist for the
 printer device

-f names an arbitrary path and -b opens it read-write, so together they
let any client that can reach the port read and append to whatever file
was named -- the enabling primitive of CVE-2018-10123.  The combination
is also legitimate (a real bidirectional printer that is not /dev/lpN),
so the runtime behaviour is left exactly as it is and the constraint is
offered as an opt-in build switch instead: define DEVICE_ALLOWLIST to a
colon separated list of device patterns, %c standing for the printer
number, e.g. -DDEVICE_ALLOWLIST='/dev/lp%c:/dev/usblp%c'.  A device
outside the list -- -f or default -- is refused at startup, before any
printer is opened.  Undefined, the check is compiled out and the build
behaves as it always has.

Only %c is honoured in a pattern; anything carrying another conversion is
treated as a configuration mistake and never matches, so a malformed
allowlist cannot make snprintf() read arguments that were not passed.

Tests: a build with the allowlist refuses a device outside it and still
accepts a listed one, and the default build is unchanged (still serves a
byte-exact job to an arbitrary -f path).  Mutation-checked: neutering
the check makes the first test fail.

Resolves U1 (design A; design B in UNRESOLVED.md is the alternative).
