After the host closed its send side the daemon keeps the job open for a
grace period, so that a printer reply emitted only then is still
forwarded.  That window was hard-wired to the 5s compile-time default and
ignored -t entirely, so every bidirectional job with a printer that never
reports end-of-stream -- a real parallel or USB printer, which simply
returns EAGAIN -- paid the full 5s on top of its actual transfer time.
Under (x)inetd, where one process serves exactly one job, that is a
several-fold slowdown of every job (measured: 0.2s of work, 5.2s wall).

The window now follows -t when a positive timeout was asked for, so a
site whose printers answer immediately can keep jobs short.  -t 0 still
falls back to the compile-time value: making the window unbounded would
let one silent printer pin a connection open forever.

Documented in p910nd.8, together with the unidirectional bound added in
the previous commit.

